  "result": "Beyond Tokens in Language Models: Interpreting Activations through Text Genre Chunks (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16540v1)\nciteturn6703view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16540v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 269\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Related Work L18:   4. cite8†3 Method L19:     1. cite9†3.1 Datasets L20:     2. cite10†3.2 Training procedure L21:     3. cite11†3.3 Dimensional Reduction L22:   5. cite12†4 Results L23:     1. cite13†4.1 Dimensional Reduction L24:     2. cite14†4.2 Labelling Accuracy L25:   6. cite15†5 Discussion and Conclusions L26:     1. cite16†5.1 Limitations L27:     2. cite17†5.2 Future Work and Broader Impacts L28:   7. cite18†References L29:   8. cite19†A Dataset Generation L30:     1. cite20†A.1 Examples L31:       1. cite21†A.1.1 Example 1: Narrative L32:     2. cite22†A.2 Example 2: Coding L33: cite23†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L34: \nL35: arXiv:2511.16540v1 [cs.CL] 20 Nov 2025\nL36: # Beyond Tokens in Language Models: Interpreting Activations through Text Genre Chunks\nL37: \nL38: Éloïse Benito-Rodriguez Independent eloise_benito@laposte.net Einar Urdshals Chalmers Technical University einarurdshals@gmail.com Jasmina Nasufi Lund University jasminanasufi9@gmail.com Nicky Pochinkov Independent paper@nicky.pro\nL39: ###### Abstract\nL40: Understanding Large Language Models (LLMs) is key to ensure their safe and beneficial deployment. This task is complicated by the difficulty of interpretability of LLM structures, and the inability to have all their outputs human-evaluated. In this paper, we present the first step towards a predictive framework, where the genre of a text used to prompt an LLM, is predicted based on its activations.\nL41: Using Mistral-7B and two datasets, we show that genre can be extracted with F1-scores of up to $98\\%$ and $71\\%$ using scikit-learn classifiers. Across both datasets, results consistently outperform the control task, providing a proof of concept that text genres can be inferred from LLMs with shallow learning models.\nL42: ## 1 Introduction\nL43: \nL44: As language models continue to improve in performance and are applied in a growing number of sectors, it is becoming more important to understand how these models work and monitor their outputs. However, the current paradigm of transformer interpretability focuses almost exclusively on predicting single tokens outputs, with some limited research looking at a specific component of text such as truthfulness or emotion.\nL45: We lay the groundwork for looking at language model outputs on the scale of clusters of tokens as interpretable natural language separations. By looking at larger blocks of text, we aim to make it easier to interpret the highest-level of predictions that a language model is making, and to make it easier to monitor large scale language models outputs.\nL46: We achieve this with two datasets. The first dataset is manually constructed using a small dataset of diverse language outputs, which we then curate using by machine- and human-labelling of different chunks of these texts. The second is an established dataset, CORE (cite24†Laippala et al., 2022 ). We show that the text chunks in these two datasets are interpretable and easy to classify using simple probes, and that these outputs may be relatively natural units of study.\nL47: We provide all code and our dataset on GitHub ^{1}^{1} 1 Code available at cite25†https://github.com/Aza-Spearal/Trajectories-Probing†github.com . .\nL48: ## 2 Related Work\nL49: \nL50: Most mechanistic interpretability research focuses on understanding the effects neuron activations have on single token activations (cite26†Geva et al., 2021 ; cite27†Chan et al., 2022 ; cite28†Conmy et al., 2023 ; cite29†nostalgebraist, 2020 ; cite30†Belrose et al., 2023 ; cite31†Olsson et al., 2022 ). While this is an important and natural bottom-up lens through which to understand models, it stands that a more top-down approach may be underexplored.\nL51: Recent work on investigating language model activations and Representation Engineering (cite32†Zou et al., 2023 ; cite33†Burns et al., 2022 ; cite34†Turner et al., 2023 ; cite35†Gurnee & Tegmark, 2023 ; cite36†Meng et al., 2022 ), suggests that it is possible to extract human-understandable concepts with their activations. These results support the hypothesis that a top-down approach to language model interpretability like the method described in this paper is tractable.\nL52: In addition, research on modularity and activation sparsity in language models (cite37†Liu et al., 2023 ; cite38†Pochinkov & Schoots, 2023 ; cite39†Zhang et al., 2022 ; cite40†Zhang et al., 2023b ; cite41†Pfeiffer et al., 2023 ), as well as the achievement of high pruning ratios for task-specific models, (cite42†Xu et al., 2022 ) suggest there may be separable ways of looking at different components of text.\nL53: Research on image models, including interpretability research (cite43†Voss et al., 2021 ; cite44†Olah et al., 2017 ; cite45†Mordvintsev et al., 2018 ), and research into the machine unlearning of specific classes and concepts (cite46†Foster et al., 2023 ; cite47†Bourtoule et al., 2021 ; cite48†Nguyen et al., 2022 ), highlights that it is possible to understand how models activate not only on the specific pixel values, but also on larger scale concepts.\nL54: Our work tries to lay the groundwork for finding these larger scale concepts in text.\nL55: There exists a large variety of research into using language models for text embedding (cite49†Muennighoff et al., 2022 ) and classification (cite50†Minaee et al., 2021 ; cite51†Howard & Ruder, 2018 ) on various datasets and tasks, including news categorisation (cite52†Zhang et al., 2015 ), sentiment analysis (cite53†Maas et al., 2011 ), question answering (cite54†Rajpurkar et al., 2016 ), semantic relatedness (cite55†Marelli et al., 2014 ), and retrieval augmentation (cite56†Thakur et al., 2021 ; cite57†Lin et al., 2022 ).\nL56: However, these are focused on using language models to perform specific tasks, rather than understanding how a language model performs tasks.\nL57: Relatedly, there is research on topic analysis of single texts (cite58†De Paoli, 2023 ) and large corpuses of text (cite59†Gauthier & Wallace, 2022 ), as well as research into qualitative deductive coding, (cite60†Zhang et al., 2023a ; cite61†Gebreegziabher et al., 2023 ; cite62†Rietz & Maedche, 2021 ).\nL58: The research is often narrow in scope, such as finding aggregate themes of outputs from specific online communities (cite63†Tai et al., 2023 ; cite64†Chew et al., 2023 ), classifying types of questions (cite65†Xiao et al., 2023 ), or extracting and reformatting knowledge (cite66†Shi et al., 2023 ).\nL59: Our research, instead of topics, aims to probe into the working of language models to understand on a high-level, how different multi-token scale components are represented in the activations, and thus investigate text categories (cite67†Drew, 2023 ) and block components.\nL60: ## 3 Method\nL61: \nL62: We define chunks as natural units of text that are split into different categories. For the most part, these chunks are analogous to paragraphs, and are simply split by double newlines \\n\\n, though with some few exceptions. For details, see Appendix cite20†A.1 .\nL63: The goal of this work is to investigate the extent to which different classifiers manage to predict the category of a text chunk, given the activations and residual stream that this text generates in a pre-trained model. To this end, we create a dataset and train various classifiers as prediction models. We describe the process and our methods in the following subsections.\nL64: ### 3.1 Datasets\nL65: \nL66: cite68†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Generation of the labelled dataset.\nL67: As shown in Figure cite69†1 , we build our dataset by creating a list of 669 prompts, which are constructed such that they can elicit varied texts from a list of categories and topics. We use them with Mistral-7B-Instruct-v2.0 (cite70†Jiang et al., 2023 ) to generate texts. These generated texts are then passed on to GPT-4 Turbo Preview (cite71†OpenAI, 2024 ), which is asked to segment the text into chunks according to categories and subsequently label each chunk with the selected category.\nL68: We obtain 3914 distinct text chunks. The categories we consider are: instructional, explanatory, speech, narrative, and code. The labelling is reviewed by humans and mislabelled text sections are removed. The prompts and examples of the labelled text are shown in the appendix.\nL69: The category distribution of this synthetic dataset is shown in Table cite72†1 L70: \nL71: Table 1: Distribution of different categories of text chunks.\nL72: Category  | instructional  | explanatory  | speech  | narrative  | code\nL73: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL74: Count  | 1159  | 699  | 548  | 542  | 290\nL75: The primary motivation for using an open-weights model like Mistral-7B-Instruct-v2.0 to generate the texts, is that it allows us to access its activations. This lays the groundwork for future work where we predict the sequence of labels based on the activations produced by the prompt.\nL76: We additionally chose a second, pre-existing classified dataset to compare our dataset agains. The chosen dataset was ‘Corpus of Online Registers of English’ (CORE) (cite24†Laippala et al., 2022 ). From the dataset, we took the overly fine-grained categories and combined them into fewer larger categories, which we used as the single labels for the entire texts. The category distribution of the CORE dataset is shown in Table cite73†2 L77: Table 2: Category distribution of the CORE dataset. Top shows category, bottom shows count.\nL78: News  | Informational  | Opinion  | Sports  | Personal  | Persuasion  | Discussion  | Instructional\nL79: Report  | Report  | Blog\nL80: 6992  | 5917  | 4594  | 1975  | 1849  | 950  | 832  | 214\nL81: ### 3.2 Training procedure\nL82: We work with the Large language model Mistral-7B (cite70†Jiang et al., 2023 ) with 7-billion parameters. The model is accessed via the Hugging Face Transformers library (cite74†Taylor et al., 2022 ) and run using PyTorch (cite75†Paszke et al., 2019 ). We prompt the LLM using text chunks from our two datasets, and we study the residual stream and activations at the output of each attention and MLP layer, as described in (cite76†Radford et al., 2019 ).\nL83: Specifically, a text chunk produces activations $a_{i}^{j}$ in layer $j$ of the LLM for chunk $c_{i}$.\nL84: cite77†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: This figure illustrates the training procedure of prediction models on the task of predicting the category of a text section. The activation $a_{i}^{j}$ of chunk $c_{i}$ in the $j$-th layer is extracted from Mistral-7B.\nL85: The means of activations $a_{i}^{j}$ serve as input for the probe models we train to predict the correct category for each labelled text chunk. We employ a range of commonly used shallow learning classifiers as our probe models from the scikit-learn library cite78†Pedregosa et al. (2011) . We call them using the Lazy Predict library cite79†Pandala & da Silva (2022) , which enables us to test several scikit-learn algorithms on the same dataset.\nL86: We keep the vanilla hyperparameters, apart from max_iter which was fixed at 100000. We scale our data by removing the mean and scaling to unit variance with StandardScaler().\nL87: Probing activations is notoriously subject to all kinds of spurious reasons for high classification accuracy (cite80†Ravichander et al., 2021 ). It demands heavy use of controls in order to validate the study. We drew inspiration from (cite81†Hewitt & Liang, 2019 ) to provide baselines: for each probe, we built a probe trained and tested on Mistral-7B activations and residual stream, but where all the parameters are random. We use the random model probe results as a control task.\nL88: If the probes on the original Mistral-7B show better performance than the control task, this will mean that our probes do not rely on spurious correlation, but on true representations in activations.\nL89: We divide our dataset into 80% for the train set and 20% for the test set.\nL90: \nL91: To evaluate our predictors, we use the Macro F1 score metric (cite82†Grandini et al., 2020 ) to give the same weight to all classes, even in an unbalanced dataset, including both recall and precision.\nL92: ### 3.3 Dimensional Reduction\nL93: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTaming Uncertainty via Automation: Observing, Analyzing, and Optimizing Agentic AI Systems (https://arxiv.org/html/2507.11277v2)\nciteturn6703view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.11277v2\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 210\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLLM-Agent-UMF: LLM-based Agent Unified Modeling Framework for Seamless Design of Multi Active/Passive Core-Agent Architectures (https://arxiv.org/html/2409.11393v3)\nciteturn6703view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2409.11393v3\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 889\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQ-MLLM: Vector Quantization for Robust Multimodal Large Language Model Security (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16229v1)\nciteturn6703view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16229v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 647\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSecure-Instruct: An Automated Pipeline for Synthesizing Instruction-Tuning Datasets Using LLMs for Secure Code Generation (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.07189v2)\nciteturn6703view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.07189v2\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 325\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReason2Attack: Jailbreaking Text-to-Image Models via LLM Reasoning (https://arxiv.org/html/2503.17987v3)\nciteturn6703view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2503.17987v3\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 393\n"
